"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,333\nEpisode 2\n\n3\n00:00:07,413 --> 00:00:09,553\nAttention is the gate\n\n4\n00:00:12,353 --> 00:00:15,056\nWatch a video of people passing a basketball,\n\n5\n00:00:15,076 --> 00:00:16,504\nand count the passes.\n\n6\n00:00:16,865 --> 00:00:21,523\nPartway through, a person in a gorilla suit walks through the middle of the scene.\n\n7\n00:00:21,948 --> 00:00:23,218\nIn the original study,\n\n8\n00:00:23,238 --> 00:00:25,457\nabout half of viewers never saw it.\n\n9\n00:00:25,871 --> 00:00:27,647\nTheir eyes were on the screen.\n\n10\n00:00:27,984 --> 00:00:29,916\nTheir attention was on the ball.\n\n11\n00:00:30,086 --> 00:00:32,172\nAttention is the gate to memory.\n\n12\n00:00:32,505 --> 00:00:34,348\nWhat you attend to can be encoded.\n\n13\n00:00:34,754 --> 00:00:37,162\nWhat you do not attend to barely gets in.\n\n14\n00:00:37,590 --> 00:00:39,739\nThe brain cannot take in everything,\n\n15\n00:00:39,759 --> 00:00:45,134\nso it selects, and what is not selected is mostly gone before memory can work on it.\n\n16\n00:00:45,304 --> 00:00:48,045\nDividing attention shows where the gate sits.\n\n17\n00:00:48,408 --> 00:00:51,467\nPeople learned word lists while doing a second task.\n\n18\n00:00:51,797 --> 00:00:55,943\nSplitting attention while learning sharply reduced what they remembered later.\n\n19\n00:00:56,375 --> 00:00:59,609\nSplitting attention while recalling barely touched memory,\n\n20\n00:00:59,823 --> 00:01:01,813\nthough it slowed the second task.\n\n21\n00:01:02,295 --> 00:01:05,882\nLearning needs attention far more than remembering does.\n\n22\n00:01:06,052 --> 00:01:08,599\nClassrooms divide attention constantly.\n\n23\n00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:10,577\nIn a simulated lecture,\n\n24\n00:01:10,848 --> 00:01:15,825\nstudents who multitasked on laptops scored eleven percent lower on a test.\n\n25\n00:01:16,289 --> 00:01:22,505\nStudents who simply had a view of a classmate's multitasking screen scored seventeen percent lower.\n\n26\n00:01:22,993 --> 00:01:25,243\nMost of them did not feel distracted.\n\n27\n00:01:25,413 --> 00:01:27,811\nAttention also drifts from the inside.\n\n28\n00:01:28,371 --> 00:01:33,429\nIn recorded lectures, mind wandering tends to rise as the lecture goes on,\n\n29\n00:01:33,669 --> 00:01:36,026\nand memory for later material falls.\n\n30\n00:01:36,418 --> 00:01:38,903\nIn live classes the picture is less clear.\n\n31\n00:01:39,450 --> 00:01:42,850\nOne study found students wandered about a third of the time,\n\n32\n00:01:43,051 --> 00:01:45,308\nwith no steady rise within a lecture,\n\n33\n00:01:45,544 --> 00:01:48,728\nand more than half of that wandering was by choice.\n\n34\n00:01:48,898 --> 00:01:49,653\nFor teaching.\n\n35\n00:01:49,964 --> 00:01:51,719\nMake the first exposure count: one demanding thing at a time.\n\n36\n00:01:51,901 --> 00:01:53,684\nSet a device policy,\n\n37\n00:01:54,019 --> 00:01:58,370\nand explain why, including the cost to neighbors.\n\n38\n00:01:58,718 --> 00:02:01,166\nTell students what to look for before you show it.\n\n39\n00:02:01,568 --> 00:02:05,187\nBreak long explanations with questions that need a response.\n\n40\n00:02:05,468 --> 00:02:08,396\nAnd remember: attention lets material in.\n\n41\n00:02:08,828 --> 00:02:11,679\nWhat students do next decides what stays.\n\n42\n00:02:11,850 --> 00:02:14,159\nNothing is remembered that never got in.\n\n43\n00:02:14,435 --> 00:02:15,505\nGuard the gate.\n\n"
